<section class="cx-problem cx-section" id="service-depth">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">What Each Service Actually Covers</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">Specifics matter.<br>Here is what is included.</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:600px">Other contractors list service names. We list what we actually do, because the difference between a good project and a frustrating one lives in the details.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-problem-grid stagger">
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Demolition</h3>
        <p>Full strip-outs, selective demo, hoarding installation, debris removal, bin management, rough cleaning, structural condition reporting, coordination with building management. We leave the space ready for the next trade, not just empty.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Steel Framing</h3>
        <p>Light gauge steel partition framing, door openings, backing at specified heights, header installation, blocking for wall-mounted items, coordination with MEP rough-in. Our framing crew is in-house, ensuring consistent quality and direct accountability throughout the project.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Drywall &amp; Ceilings</h3>
        <p>Hang, tape, and multi-coat finish. Level 4 finish as standard on commercial applications. T-bar ceiling grids, bulkheads, soffits, acoustic tile, specialty ceilings. Every coat is applied and sanded to specification before the next trade steps in.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Finishing</h3>
        <p>Primer, paint (2-coat minimum), accent walls, trim installation, base and door casing, LVP and carpet flooring, tile, epoxy floors for industrial applications. Finish schedule managed against the approved specifications.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Millwork &amp; Installation</h3>
        <p>Custom reception desks, office built-ins, break room cabinetry, credenzas, shelving, feature walls, signage substrates. Shop fabricated to spec and scheduled into the project timeline from day one so installation lands exactly when the sequence requires it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Trade Coordination</h3>
        <p>Full MEP sequencing, sub scheduling and re-scheduling, daily site management, permit management, inspection coordination, conflict resolution, progress reporting. The work does not fall through the cracks because one person owns the whole picture.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- COST GUIDE -->
<section class="cx-cost-guide cx-section" id="cost-guide">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">Commercial Renovation Cost Guide: Toronto &amp; GTA</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">What does commercial renovation<br>cost in the GTA?</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:680px">This is one of the most searched questions in our industry and one of the most poorly answered. The honest answer is that cost depends on scope, condition, finishes, and timeline. Here is a framework for understanding it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-cost-ranges stagger">
      <div class="cx-cost-range r">
        <div class="cx-cost-range-type">Office Tenant Improvement</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-val">$60–$120 / sq ft</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-note">Open-to-enclosed TI. Shell to turnkey, full MEP, drywall, ceilings, millwork. Spec finish level and MEP density are the primary cost drivers.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-cost-range r">
        <div class="cx-cost-range-type">Medical Office Fit-Out</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-val">$120–$220 / sq ft</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-note">Higher plumbing density, specialized HVAC, AODA compliance, clinical-grade finishes. Regulatory requirements drive cost well above standard office.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-cost-range r">
        <div class="cx-cost-range-type">Restaurant Renovation</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-val">$150–$300 / sq ft</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-note">Commercial kitchen MEP, hood systems, grease trap rough-in, health unit compliance, high-end FOH finishes. Kitchen scope is the biggest variable.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-cost-range r">
        <div class="cx-cost-range-type">Retail / Light Commercial</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-val">$40–$90 / sq ft</div>
        <div class="cx-cost-range-note">Branded fit-outs, storefront builds, light commercial. Cost depends on finish level and whether structural or MEP changes are involved.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-cost-factors stagger" style="margin-top:2px">
      <div class="cx-cost-factor r">
        <h3>What Drives Cost Up</h3>
        <p>High plumbing density (medical, restaurant), specialty HVAC requirements, level 5 drywall finish, full custom millwork, tight timelines requiring weekend crews, phased builds in occupied spaces, extensive permit management, structural modifications, and specialty materials with long lead times.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-cost-factor r">
        <h3>What Drives Cost Down</h3>
        <p>Simple open-plan layouts, standard finish specifications, minimal MEP changes, good base building condition with accurate drawings, generous timelines, flexible scheduling, and early engagement that allows proper long-lead item ordering. The biggest cost savings come from planning, not cutting scope.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-cost-factor r">
        <h3>How VNG Quotes</h3>
        <p>Our quotes are broken out by trade, with every line item priced to actual cost. Exclusions are listed explicitly. Items that could affect scope, including slab conditions, existing MEP, and permit comments, are flagged at quote time, not after signing. You get a number you can plan around, and it does not move unless the scope moves. When scope changes are needed, you approve them in writing before we proceed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

